+An example of creating a model file and defining a collective variable component using \texttt{torchANN} is shown below:
+
+\begin{mdexampleinput}
+\-import torch\\
+\-import torch.nn as nn\\
+\\
+\-\#~The~neural~network~model is a function: \\
+\-\#~~~~~$f=(f_1,f_2,f_3): \mathbb{R}^8\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^3$.\\
+\-model = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(8, 100), \\
+\-~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~nn.ReLU(), \\
+\-~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~nn.Linear(100, 3))\\
+\#~Train the model\\
+\-...\\
+\#~Save~the~trained~model\\
+\-torch.jit.script(model).save("./model.pt")
+\end{mdexampleinput}
+
+\begin{cvexampleinput}
+\-colvar~\{\\
+\-~~\#~Define a scalar CV as $\xi=f_1(x_2,y_2,z_2,x_4,y_4,z_4, \phi, \psi)$, \\
+\-~~\#~i.e.~the function represented by the first component of the Torch model, \\
+\-~~\#~evaluated at the coordinates of the two atoms with atomNumbers 2 and 4,\\
+\-~~\#~and the two torsion angles $\phi,\psi$.\\
+\-~~name~nn\_output\_1\\
+\-~~torchann~\{\\
+\-~~~~modelFile~model.pt\\
+\-~~~~output\_component 0\\
+\-~~~~cartesian~\{\\
+\-~~~~~~atoms~\{\\
+\-~~~~~~~~atomNumbers \{\{2 4\}\}\\
+\-~~~~~~\}\\
+\-~~~~\}\\ 
+\-~~~~dihedral~\{\\
+\-~~~~~~name~001\\
+\-~~~~~~group1~\{atomnumbers~\{5\}\}\\
+\-~~~~~~group2~\{atomnumbers~\{7\}\}\\
+\-~~~~~~group3~\{atomnumbers~\{9\}\}\\
+\-~~~~~~group4~\{atomnumbers~\{15\}\}\\
+\-~~~~\}\\
+\-~~~~dihedral~\{\\
+\-~~~~~~name~002\\
+\-~~~~~~group1~\{atomnumbers~\{7\}\}\\
+\-~~~~~~group2~\{atomnumbers~\{9\}\}\\
+\-~~~~~~group3~\{atomnumbers~\{15\}\}\\
+\-~~~~~~group4~\{atomnumbers~\{17\}\}\\
+\-~~~~\}\\
+\-~~\}\\
+\-\}
+\end{cvexampleinput}
 
